  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 228 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nLike the previous AFD mentioned, our forecast continues to feature \nreduced rain chances on Sunday, with the most likely scenario being \nshowers confined to the western half or so of the forecast area. \nWhy? The global ensembles depict strong surface high pressure \nanchored across the Eastern Seaboard through the day, with a passing \nshortwave trough aloft. A \"wedge\" will result from this setup, with \nmoisture advection atop supporting the potential for the \naforementioned showers (or drizzle, per some forecast soundings) on \nSunday, and the potential shifting across the rest of the state \nSunday night into Monday morning. The chances for rain are \nessentially nil on Monday and Tuesday as persistent regional high \npressure reinforces the wedge across north and central Georgia.\n\nThe synoptic scale pattern will change at midweek. Longwave \ntroughing will set up aloft over the Eastern CONUS Wednesday and \nThursday, but there are differences among the global ensembles \nregarding the strength of the troughing, plus jet streak \nplacement/where wind maxima set up aloft (as the previous AFD \ndiscussed). This will impact the footprint of resulting surface low \npressure and thus where rainfall is maximized. At this time, there \nis general consensus among the global ensembles that total rainfall \nwill be maximized roughly along and north of the I-85 corridor, with \ntotals ranging from 1 to 3 inches. That said, this could change as \nthe aforementioned uncertain variables become better handled by the \nglobal and eventually regional/convective-allowing models. These \nvariables will also impact parameters (such as deep-layer shear) \nthat govern the potential for strong to severe storms. For now, it \nis safe to say that Wednesday and Thursday could be rainy and cloudy \nfor some of north and central Georgia.",
